indium (1.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Inspector shows hex. Selecting a file that is neither text nor an image INDIUM
    can decode used to produce a sentence explaining that a hex view would arrive in
    version 1.1; it now produces the hex view. Sixteen bytes to a row, the offset down
    the left and the printable characters down the right, exactly as xxd prints them, so
    an offset you read here is the offset you would read anywhere else.
  * The row is sixteen bytes wide whatever the width of the pane. Dragging the Inspector
    wider shows more of each line rather than rearranging the file, so a byte keeps its
    offset while you look at it. The pane scrolls sideways for the rest.
  * It stays quick on a large file. Only the rows on screen are built, so a megabyte of
    binary costs what a handful of lines costs.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 11 Aug 2026 10:55:00 +0300

indium (1.0.0-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Inspector no longer shows a file the way it used to be. Applying a
    staged change rebuilds the archive and re-opens it, but the Preview pane
    kept whatever it was already showing — so replacing a file and pressing
    Apply left the old contents, and the old picture, on screen beside the new
    archive. The checksum was being refreshed all along; the preview now goes
    with it.
  * Every popup's footer reaches its own edges. The band across the foot fell
    two points short on the left, the right and the bottom, leaving a thin
    border of the popup's own colour around what should have been a full-width
    band.
  * The status bar's numbers no longer slide about while an archive is being
    read. The entry count and the sizes grow as entries stream in, and each
    fresh digit pushed the ratio a step to the right; every number now sits in
    a fixed column and stays there.
  * Two archives whose names differ only outside ASCII no longer share one
    lock. açık.zip and aşık.zip in one folder were treated as the same file, so
    opening both and applying a change to either reported that another INDIUM
    window was already rebuilding it.
  * The build now tests itself on every push, not only when a release is cut.
    The checks are the same ones the release has always run.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 11 Aug 2026 09:35:00 +0300

indium (1.0.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Extraction no longer reports success for files it never wrote. libarchive
    answers ARCHIVE_WARN both for a file it wrote but could not finish stamping
    and for one it could not create at all, and INDIUM counted the second as
    written — so extracting into a directory you cannot write said "Extracted 1
    entry." over an empty folder. It now asks the filesystem whether the file
    arrived, and says plainly when it did not.
  * Every failure INDIUM reports is now yellow and carries a warning triangle.
    Seven messages were the same grey as a confirmation, including every
    extraction failure and every archive that could not be opened.
  * Icons. The sidebar and the status bar carry glyphs from the typeface already
    embedded in the binary — no new asset, no new dependency, and a test that
    fails the build rather than let one render as an empty box.
  * The sidebar is ordered the way a person reads it: Open file and Archive
    above a rule, Bookmarks and Recent files below it. F1 lists every key, and a
    test holds that list to the design document.
  * The keyboard's place in a list is a line rather than a colour, so it can be
    seen on a row that is also selected. Rules and separators are legible at
    last. Buttons are capsules. The typeface is Fira Mono Nerd Font.
  * The status bar's directory is shortened from the middle, so both the tree it
    lives in and the folder it sits in survive; hovering shows it whole, and
    clicking it opens that folder in your file manager.
  * Long operations draw their progress as a line along the top edge of the
    status bar instead of a bar inside it. Cancel has not moved.
  * Resizing the window no longer snaps. INDIUM declared a minimum larger than
    the window some compositors hand it, so the first pixel of a drag jumped the
    frame; and the entry table's columns had stopped following the window
    entirely, leaving dead space beside the last one.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 23:09:24 +0300

indium (1.0.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every filename is read correctly, whatever alphabet it is in. libarchive
    converts a stored name into the current locale's character set as it reads
    the header, and a Rust program never calls setlocale — so INDIUM ran in the
    C locale, any name with a byte outside ASCII failed to convert, and the
    entry arrived with no name at all. A nameless entry matched no selection,
    so extraction and copy-out skipped it in silence. Every kopek.txt,
    resume.pdf and Japanese filename in every archive has been left behind
    since the first release. An archive INDIUM cannot name every member of is
    now refused outright rather than partly extracted.
  * Ctrl+V really does stage files now. 1.0.0-2 claimed this and shipped only
    half of it: the toolkit swallows the paste chord whether or not it has any
    text to hand back, and a file manager copying a file offers no plain text
    at all, so nothing was emitted and nothing could be noticed. The chord is
    read when the key comes back up, which survives every clipboard state.
  * Ctrl+C no longer starts an extraction while text in the status bar is
    selected.
  * Add files... beside the breadcrumb, and Browse... in the Ctrl+O popup, both
    raising the desktop's own file picker through xdg-desktop-portal. Dragging
    files onto the window needs X11 and has never worked on Wayland: winit
    implements no Wayland drag-and-drop.
  * One click opens a recent file. The third bookmark stays highlighted, Enter
    on a recent opens it, and each sidebar section keeps its own cursor.
  * The confirm-password box accepts typing, arrow keys choose an application
    in Open With, and Tab completion leaves the caret at the end of the path.
  * The sidebar no longer draws over itself in a short window, and
    build/install-desktop.sh runs from any directory.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 14:39:58 +0300

indium (1.0.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V work. In 1.0.0-1 neither did anything at all: the toolkit
    turns a clipboard chord into a clipboard event and never emits the key, and
    INDIUM was watching for the key. Ctrl+C is copy-out's only entry point, so
    copying entries out of an archive and pasting files into one were both
    unreachable rather than merely awkward. Dropping files onto the window came
    in on a different path and was never affected.
  * No other change to the program. The version is unchanged and the revision
    is bumped instead, at the maker's word: 1.0.0-2 is a different binary from
    1.0.0-1, which is not what a Debian revision usually means.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 08:48:44 +0300

indium (1.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release. INDIUM is an archive manager for Linux on Wayland, shaped
    around one idea: the metadata is the main event. A permanent Inspector pane
    shows sizes, packed sizes, ratio, method, checksums, four timestamps,
    ownership, mode, link targets and encryption state, instead of hiding all of
    it behind a Properties dialog.
  * All format work happens inside the process. INDIUM never runs 7z, tar,
    unzip, zstd or any other external compressor — every supported format is
    supported by code linked into this binary, on every machine, whether or not
    an archive tool is installed.
  * Reads every container and filter system libarchive reads; writes tar with
    gz, bz2, xz, zst or lz4, zip with Deflate, and 7z with LZMA2. Encryption is
    7z AES-256 and nothing else.
  * RAR is deliberately absent — not read, not written. Opening one produces a
    plain sentence and no half-support.
  * Wayland only, and toolkit-free: no GTK, no Qt, no KF6, no portal, no X11.
    No network of any kind — no update check, no telemetry, no crash reporting.
  * Packaged without dpkg-deb. The build machine is Arch and has none; a .deb is
    an ar archive of three members, so build/package/make-deb.sh writes it out.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 01:05:57 +0300
